1
00:00:02,199 --> 00:00:05,089
Let's understand how requests reach the

2
00:00:05,089 --> 00:00:07,225
server and how we send back responses.

3
00:00:08,317 --> 00:00:10,292
So we got an incoming request on the server,

4
00:00:10,709 --> 00:00:13,118
and from that server, as you just saw, you

5
00:00:13,118 --> 00:00:15,173
can extract various kinds of information.

6
00:00:15,687 --> 00:00:17,549
You can extract the headers, but you can

7
00:00:17,598 --> 00:00:20,151
also find out what the method or the URL is.

8
00:00:21,467 --> 00:00:22,977
Now then, as a next step,

9
00:00:23,555 --> 00:00:24,775
and that's what we're going to do in a

10
00:00:24,775 --> 00:00:27,569
second, You can extract the body of the

11
00:00:27,569 --> 00:00:30,748
request, so any data that might be attached

12
00:00:30,844 --> 00:00:31,679
to the request.

13
00:00:32,209 --> 00:00:35,244
And I'm saying might because requests don't

14
00:00:35,276 --> 00:00:37,813
need to carry data. They can, but they don't

15
00:00:37,829 --> 00:00:38,262
have to.

16
00:00:39,531 --> 00:00:41,104
Important here at this point

17
00:00:41,682 --> 00:00:44,557
is that the body, the data, is actually

18
00:00:44,653 --> 00:00:47,415
being streamed in. So when the request hits

19
00:00:47,479 --> 00:00:48,089
the server,

20
00:00:48,667 --> 00:00:50,706
the data might not be fully there yet.

21
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:53,816
Instead, it's streamed in chunk by chunk

22
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:56,924
and therefore our request handling logic

23
00:00:57,180 --> 00:01:00,193
needs to be able to deal with that stream of

24
00:01:00,289 --> 00:01:00,625
data.

25
00:01:01,907 --> 00:01:02,821
Once we got the

26
00:01:03,349 --> 00:01:05,513
data, once all the data is there and we

27
00:01:05,513 --> 00:01:06,121
parsed it,

28
00:01:06,698 --> 00:01:09,518
we can run our core logic. We can, for

29
00:01:09,518 --> 00:01:12,226
example, reach out to a database or to a

30
00:01:12,226 --> 00:01:14,950
file to store the data in there if that's

31
00:01:14,950 --> 00:01:15,623
what we wanna do.

32
00:01:16,649 --> 00:01:18,828
And then we can prepare the response. Set up

33
00:01:18,877 --> 00:01:21,236
the body of the response and any metadata

34
00:01:21,236 --> 00:01:23,628
like headers that should be added to the

35
00:01:23,628 --> 00:01:24,254
response

36
00:01:24,816 --> 00:01:27,047
and send the response back to the client.

37
00:01:27,946 --> 00:01:30,097
This is how requests and responses are

38
00:01:30,193 --> 00:01:31,349
handled on the server.

39
00:01:32,039 --> 00:01:34,591
Let's now take care about that body parsing.

40
00:01:35,651 --> 00:01:36,758
To parse the body

41
00:01:37,577 --> 00:01:39,808
on the request, we got that body

42
00:01:40,562 --> 00:01:41,092
property

43
00:01:41,831 --> 00:01:42,376
and in

44
00:01:42,954 --> 00:01:45,330
the hint here, we even got a snippet on how

45
00:01:45,394 --> 00:01:47,995
we could parse this. And this is roughly

46
00:01:48,172 --> 00:01:49,649
what we're going to rebuild here

47
00:01:50,355 --> 00:01:51,206
because the body

48
00:01:51,945 --> 00:01:54,771
object which we actually got here has a read

49
00:01:54,771 --> 00:01:57,147
method because as I said, it's a stream of

50
00:01:57,179 --> 00:02:01,193
data and the read method wants a Uint8Array

51
00:02:01,739 --> 00:02:02,526
as a place

52
00:02:03,184 --> 00:02:05,432
where it stores the incoming bytes.

53
00:02:06,251 --> 00:02:08,066
And this actually is something which you

54
00:02:08,082 --> 00:02:09,479
kind of already saw

55
00:02:10,249 --> 00:02:11,807
in the previous course module.

56
00:02:12,657 --> 00:02:14,916
There I showed you how you can read user

57
00:02:14,996 --> 00:02:16,823
input coming from the console, from the

58
00:02:16,823 --> 00:02:17,256
terminal.

59
00:02:18,186 --> 00:02:22,320
Now there we also used uint8 array to store

60
00:02:22,512 --> 00:02:25,061
the incoming bytes in such an array and then

61
00:02:25,093 --> 00:02:27,080
convert that to a string eventually.

62
00:02:27,817 --> 00:02:30,558
That's why I did show you these basics first

63
00:02:30,942 --> 00:02:33,891
so that we now also understand this next

64
00:02:33,891 --> 00:02:34,468
step here.

65
00:02:35,269 --> 00:02:37,192
Now here, of course, we're not reading data

66
00:02:37,192 --> 00:02:39,500
the user entered into the terminal, but

67
00:02:39,516 --> 00:02:42,288
instead we're reading the incoming chunks of

68
00:02:42,321 --> 00:02:44,952
data which we're receiving as part of the

69
00:02:44,952 --> 00:02:45,963
incoming request.

70
00:02:47,250 --> 00:02:49,695
So therefore, we now need such a uint8 array

71
00:02:49,775 --> 00:02:51,384
to store the request data.

72
00:02:52,044 --> 00:02:53,347
So I'll create my buffer here,

73
00:02:53,942 --> 00:02:54,232
a new

74
00:02:54,831 --> 00:02:56,185
uint8 array.

75
00:02:57,866 --> 00:02:59,628
We can pass the buffer to read.

76
00:03:00,702 --> 00:03:03,637
Now we should allocate enough memory though.

77
00:03:03,864 --> 00:03:06,572
We should let the uint8 array constructor

78
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:08,128
know how long

79
00:03:08,732 --> 00:03:11,429
this stream of data will be, so how many

80
00:03:11,461 --> 00:03:12,953
bytes we plan on storing.

81
00:03:13,788 --> 00:03:16,516
And thankfully, we get this information on

82
00:03:16,516 --> 00:03:17,608
the request

83
00:03:18,282 --> 00:03:22,358
content-length field. This tells us how big

84
00:03:22,374 --> 00:03:24,830
the request data is, essentially how much

85
00:03:24,862 --> 00:03:27,430
space we need to reserve. And therefore,

86
00:03:27,510 --> 00:03:29,083
this works together quite well.

87
00:03:29,998 --> 00:03:31,651
You can tell, however, that I'm getting an

88
00:03:31,828 --> 00:03:32,197
error here,

89
00:03:32,887 --> 00:03:35,476
and the core problem is that content-length

90
00:03:35,686 --> 00:03:37,250
actually is a union type.

91
00:03:37,798 --> 00:03:38,895
It's either a number

92
00:03:39,476 --> 00:03:39,879
or null.

93
00:03:40,540 --> 00:03:42,717
It's null if we got no information about the

94
00:03:42,717 --> 00:03:44,798
content.
